messages:
anya: hey team — hit a weird repro in the go sample app. when we send a SIGINT during a long-running generator stream, the process doesn't exit (goroutine leak). minimal repro attached below. anyone seen auth refresh + ctx cancellation interact like this?

matt: can you paste the snippet?

anya: sure — trimmed version from sampleapp: 
```go
func streamLoop(ctx context.Context) error {
    for {
        res, err := client.Generate(ctx, req) // streaming generator
        if err != nil {
            return err
        }
        // process tokens...
    }
}

func start() {
    ctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())
    go func() {
        sig := make(chan os.Signal, 1)
        signal.Notify(sig, os.Interrupt)
        <-sig
        cancel()
    }()

    go authRefresher(ctx) // spawns background refresh loop
    streamLoop(ctx)
}

func authRefresher(ctx context.Context) {
    for {
        token := getTokenBlocking() // blocks on network + internal mutex
        select {
        case <-time.After(30 * time.Second):
            _ = token
        case <-ctx.Done():
            return
        }
    }
}
```

anya: behavior: send SIGINT, main returns from streamLoop but process stays alive. pprof shows goroutine stuck inside getTokenBlocking (inside refresh HTTP call) with no ctx propagation.

sam: yeah that matches a pattern: refresh goroutine blocks while holding some mutex/conn and doesn't get cancelled because getTokenBlocking doesn't take ctx.

jenna: we recommend the SDK provide a RefreshWithContext(ctx) or accept a context on the token source. otherwise app-level signal handling is fragile.

anya: agreed. in the sample app we spawned authRefresher(ctx) assuming cancel would bubble but internal token retrieval is non-cancellable.

matt: quick mitigation: make the refresh use the same http.Client with ctx-aware req, or wrap getTokenBlocking with a goroutine + select on ctx and a channel to abort. ugly but works.

sam: for the SDK side — do we have a pattern in go-client for context-aware token refresh? I remember some discussion about not forcing ctx on interfaces because it changes semantics.

jenna: we opted to keep TokenSource interface simple historically, but several customers ran into this. The current plan from auth team was to add an optional Refresh(ctx) method and default to Refresh() for backcompat, but we never landed it in the go SDK.

anya: would an SDK change unblock most folks? feels like onboarding friction: sample apps show naive goroutine that cancels but still leaks.

matt: yes. two low-effort fixes:
- update sampleapp to use signal.NotifyContext instead of manual channel + ensure refresh uses context-aware http call.
- add SDK helper NewContextAwareTokenSource(ctx, ts) that wraps old TokenSource and cancels blocking refresh calls by doing the request with ctx.

sam: also add a short note in the README "long-running streams + token refresh: ensure token refresh is cancellable or run with process lifetime management". small docs patch helps UX.

anya: i'll open a tiny PR for the sampleapp and a ticket for SDK change. tagging @matt for review.

matt: :thumbsup: if you can include a small unit test that simulates a blocking refresh (use a test server that stalls) that'll make the SDK change more convincing.

ci-bot: Created task RW-4217: "go sdk: context-aware auth refresh for long-running streams" and triggered e2e with flaky=false.

jenna: thanks — also capture this in the generator onboarding guide (short checklist item). we keep getting these reports from new users running embedded/on-prem pods.

anya: filing repro + attaching flamegraph. will follow up with PR link once ready. thanks for quick triage!"
